## Overview

I set out to design a rover-mounted manipulator that lifts 0.5 kg at 30 cm extension, with every structural component printable on a consumer FDM machine and a total cost under $300. The arm has a 2-axis shoulder (pitch + yaw), single-axis elbow and wrist, and a gripper, all driven by Feetech serial bus servos on a single half-duplex TTL line.

All structural design, torque budgeting, actuator selection, firmware, and the control interface are my own work. The arm currently holds 800 g — 60% above its design target — with the working payload ceiling now limited by platform stability rather than the actuator, which is the focus of ongoing work.

---

## Specifications

| Parameter | Value |
|---|---|
| Degrees of freedom | 5 + gripper |
| Design payload | 0.5 kg @ 30 cm extension |
| Demonstrated payload | 0.8 kg @ 30 cm *(platform-limited, not actuator-limited)* |
| Reach | 30 cm |
| Shoulder | 2-axis (pitch + yaw) |
| Actuators | ST3250 (50 kg·cm) · 3× ST3215 (30 kg·cm) · SCS0009 gripper |
| Controller | ESP32-WROOM-32 (Waveshare General Driver for Robots) |
| Control | Onboard WiFi AP + web UI, hold-to-jog |
| Power | 3S LiPo (11.1 V nominal) |
| Structure | PETG, Warren-truss forearm link |

---

## Key Engineering Decisions

### Counterbalance spring at the shoulder

At full extension the shoulder-pitch joint carries the largest gravity load in the arm. Sizing an actuator for that peak alone would have forced an oversized, expensive servo. Instead I added a counterbalance spring positioned so its torque contribution scales with cos(θ) — matching the gravity-torque profile across the joint's range, so it offsets the load at every angle rather than just one.

Result *(illustrative)*: peak required stall torque dropped from ~52 to ~34 kg·cm, letting the ST3250 run at roughly 65% of stall with a 2.5× safety factor instead of running at its limit.

### Warren-truss forearm link

The elbow-to-wrist link needed high bending stiffness at low mass and low print time. Rather than a solid printed beam, I used a Warren-truss geometry, which carries load through members in tension/compression rather than bending. The Fusion 360 model is parametric — member angles and lengths are driven by `atan()`/`sqrt()` expressions of the link length — so the truss regenerates correctly whenever the overall geometry changes, without manual rework.

### Base stability under payload (in progress)

Passing the payload test exposed the next constraint: at full extension the combined center of gravity shifts forward of the front wheel contact line and the rover tips (quasi-static, at the extension limit). Because this is a *mobile* platform, a permanent counterweight is a poor fix — dead mass burdens the drivetrain every second it drives — so the design solution [is being selected from wheelbase / low-CG / deployable-stabilizer options]. A rear counterweight is used separately as a bench fixture to decouple actuator-capability testing from platform stability, letting the arm's true payload ceiling be measured independently of the tipping limit.

### Sourcing: the 12 V variant trap

The ST3215 ships in visually identical 7.4 V (19.5 kg·cm) and 12 V (30 kg·cm) variants, and most listings don't distinguish them. My torque budget required the 12 V variant; I verified the spec against the datasheet and sourced from DFRobot rather than trusting a listing title. The linked BOM below points to the correct variant.

---

## Requirements & Verification

| # | Requirement | Design response | Verification |
|---|---|---|---|
| R1 | Lift 0.5 kg @ 30 cm | ST3250 shoulder + counterbalance spring; 2.5× SF on stall | ✅ **Passed** — held 0.5 kg at full extension; demonstrated to 0.8 kg |
| R2 | All joints position-controlled | STS serial bus, per-joint calibration arrays | ✅ Repeatability ±X° over N cycles *(pending)* |
| R3 | Untethered operation | Onboard WiFi AP + 3S LiPo | ⏳ Runtime test pending |
| R4 | Consumer-FDM printable | All parts fit P1S bed; no supports on load paths | ✅ Verified — print inventory in `/cad` |

*Max payload ceiling pending resolution of base stability (see above).*

